    (v_topic_id, 'A 56-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the pelvis. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 7-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the femur that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Radiation therapy", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Intra-articular steroid injection", "Cryoablation without imaging", "Open en bloc resection"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal humerus. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Radiation therapy", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Amputation", "Bisphosphonates", "Denosumab"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male presents with severe back pain. Radiographs of the lumbar spine show a dense, sclerotic (osteoblastic) lesion in the L3 vertebral body. Laboratory studies show an elevated alkaline phosphatase, but normal calcium. Which primary malignancy is most likely responsible for this metastatic lesion?', '["Malignant melanoma", "Multiple myeloma", "Thyroid carcinoma", "Prostate carcinoma", "Renal cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Metastatic prostate cancer characteristically produces osteoblastic (sclerotic) bone lesions due to the secretion of factors that stimulate bone formation (like BMPs). Conversely, cancers like renal cell, thyroid, and multiple myeloma typically cause osteolytic (destructive) lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 7-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the tibia that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Open en bloc resection", "Cryoablation without imaging", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Intra-articular steroid injection", "Radiation therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
