    (v_topic_id, 'A 58-year-old patient presents with a slowly worsening ache in the proximal femur. Radiographs show a large, poorly defined intramedullary lesion with endosteal scalloping and prominent ''popcorn'' or ''ring-and-arc'' calcifications. Biopsy confirms malignant cartilage. What is the primary modality of treatment?', '["Systemic chemotherapy", "Wide surgical resection", "High-dose radiation therapy", "Denosumab therapy", "Radiofrequency ablation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Chondrosarcomas are malignant tumors of cartilage typically seen in older adults (pelvis, proximal femur). They are notoriously resistant to both chemotherapy and radiation therapy. Therefore, wide surgical excision is the mainstay of treatment for conventional chondrosarcomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old male presents with severe back pain. Radiographs of the lumbar spine show a dense, sclerotic (osteoblastic) lesion in the L3 vertebral body. Laboratory studies show an elevated alkaline phosphatase, but normal calcium. Which primary malignancy is most likely responsible for this metastatic lesion?', '["Malignant melanoma", "Thyroid carcinoma", "Prostate carcinoma", "Renal cell carcinoma", "Multiple myeloma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Metastatic prostate cancer characteristically produces osteoblastic (sclerotic) bone lesions due to the secretion of factors that stimulate bone formation (like BMPs). Conversely, cancers like renal cell, thyroid, and multiple myeloma typically cause osteolytic (destructive) lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 36-year-old patient presents with a swollen, deformed hand. Radiographs show multiple centrally located radiolucent lesions with stippled calcifications within the phalanges and metacarpals. Physical exam reveals multiple soft tissue hemangiomas on the same limb. What is the diagnosis?', '["Multiple hereditary exostoses", "McCune-Albright syndrome", "Ollier disease", "Neurofibromatosis type 1", "Maffucci syndrome"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Enchondromas are benign cartilage tumors of the medullary cavity, frequently found in the small bones of the hands and feet. Multiple enchondromas alone characterize Ollier disease. When multiple enchondromas are accompanied by soft tissue hemangiomas, it is called Maffucci syndrome, which carries a much higher risk of malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma and other visceral malignancies.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 15-year-old presents with a painful, enlarging mass around the proximal humerus. Radiographs reveal a destructive, mixed lytic and blastic lesion in the metaphysis, with a ''sunburst'' periosteal reaction and a Codman triangle. A biopsy shows malignant cells producing unmineralized osteoid. Which of the following is the standard treatment protocol?', '["Surgical resection alone", "Curettage, phenol adjuvant, and cementation", "Primary radiation therapy followed by resection", "Neoadjuvant radiation and chemotherapy without surgery", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy, surgical resection, and adjuvant chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Osteosarcoma is the most common primary bone malignancy in adolescents. It typically occurs in the metaphysis of long bones. Histologic hallmark is the production of osteoid by malignant cells. Standard treatment is neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, wide surgical resection (limb salvage), followed by adjuvant chemotherapy based on tumor necrosis rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the pelvis. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Osteoblast overactivation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 30-year-old patient requires an open incisional biopsy for a suspected high-grade soft tissue sarcoma of the anterior thigh. Which of the following is a critical principle when performing this biopsy?', '["Use a longitudinal incision placed within the planned definitive resection ellipse", "Dissect extensively through intermuscular planes to avoid muscle damage", "Obtain a small sample of the necrotic center rather than the periphery to ensure a diagnosis", "Use a transverse incision parallel to skin tension lines for a better cosmetic result", "Exsanguinate the limb with an Esmarch bandage before inflating the tourniquet"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Orthopedic oncology biopsy principles: 1) Longitudinal incisions (transverse incisions contaminate more tissue longitudinally). 2) The biopsy tract must be excisable en bloc with the tumor. 3) Avoid spreading through fascial planes (split muscle instead). 4) Do NOT exsanguinate with an Esmarch to prevent pushing tumor cells centrally. 5) Biopsy the periphery (the advancing edge), as the center is often necrotic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the pelvis. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Separation of cellular blood components from serum", "Fat floating on water within a lipoma", "Pus settling from an abscess", "Contrast pooling in a hemangioma", "Necrotic tumor debris"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal pelvis. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 10-year-old child presents with a 1-month history of fever, localized pain, and swelling over the mid-shaft of the femur. Labs show an elevated ESR and leukocytosis. Radiographs demonstrate a permeative, lytic lesion in the diaphysis with a multilamellated ''onion-skin'' periosteal reaction. Which chromosomal translocation is most characteristically associated with this diagnosis?', '["t(8;14)", "t(X;18)", "t(9;22)", "t(11;22)", "t(14;18)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ewing sarcoma often mimics osteomyelitis clinically (fever, elevated inflammatory markers). It occurs in the diaphysis of long bones. It is a small round blue cell tumor strongly associated with the t(11;22) translocation, resulting in the EWS-FLI1 fusion protein. CD99 is typically strongly positive.'),
