    (v_topic_id, 'A 49-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal pelvis. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 64-year-old male presents with severe back pain. Radiographs of the lumbar spine show a dense, sclerotic (osteoblastic) lesion in the L3 vertebral body. Laboratory studies show an elevated alkaline phosphatase, but normal calcium. Which primary malignancy is most likely responsible for this metastatic lesion?', '["Malignant melanoma", "Renal cell carcinoma", "Multiple myeloma", "Prostate carcinoma", "Thyroid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Metastatic prostate cancer characteristically produces osteoblastic (sclerotic) bone lesions due to the secretion of factors that stimulate bone formation (like BMPs). Conversely, cancers like renal cell, thyroid, and multiple myeloma typically cause osteolytic (destructive) lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 16-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal tibia. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Radiation therapy", "Amputation", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Bisphosphonates", "Denosumab"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal tibia. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Denosumab", "Bisphosphonates", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Radiation therapy", "Amputation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 34-year-old patient presents with a slowly growing, deep, painless mass in the popliteal fossa. MRI shows a heterogeneous mass near the knee joint, but not within the joint capsule. Biopsy confirms a soft tissue sarcoma. Genetic testing reveals a t(X;18) chromosomal translocation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Rhabdomyosarcoma", "Synovial Sarcoma", "Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma", "Liposarcoma", "Clear cell sarcoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Despite its name, Synovial Sarcoma rarely arises from the actual synovium of the joint, but typically occurs near large joints (e.g., the knee) in young adults. It is strongly associated with the t(X;18) translocation, which creates the SS18-SSX fusion gene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 13-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the pelvis that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Open en bloc resection", "Radiation therapy", "Cryoablation without imaging", "Intra-articular steroid injection"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old patient requires an open incisional biopsy for a suspected high-grade soft tissue sarcoma of the anterior thigh. Which of the following is a critical principle when performing this biopsy?', '["Obtain a small sample of the necrotic center rather than the periphery to ensure a diagnosis", "Use a longitudinal incision placed within the planned definitive resection ellipse", "Use a transverse incision parallel to skin tension lines for a better cosmetic result", "Exsanguinate the limb with an Esmarch bandage before inflating the tourniquet", "Dissect extensively through intermuscular planes to avoid muscle damage"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Orthopedic oncology biopsy principles: 1) Longitudinal incisions (transverse incisions contaminate more tissue longitudinally). 2) The biopsy tract must be excisable en bloc with the tumor. 3) Avoid spreading through fascial planes (split muscle instead). 4) Do NOT exsanguinate with an Esmarch to prevent pushing tumor cells centrally. 5) Biopsy the periphery (the advancing edge), as the center is often necrotic.');
